Features DXVK asks for, when FineWine runs Direct3D 9 on it

MoltenVK/MoltenVK/GPUObjects/MVKDevice.mm
- DXVK refuses to create a device unless the GPU reports geometry shaders and cull distances
  (it also asks for 64-bit floats and depth clamping where reported). Metal has no geometry
  shaders, and MoltenVK says so. FineWine uses DXVK for Direct3D 9 only, which has neither
  geometry shaders nor cull distances, so with FINEWINE_DXVK_FEATURES=1 in the environment
  the features are reported. FineWine sets the variable only for programs it runs on DXVK
  (the prefix setting "Direct3D 9 and older on Vulkan"); every other program sees what Metal
  really has.

diff --git a/MoltenVK/MoltenVK/GPUObjects/MVKDevice.mm b/MoltenVK/MoltenVK/GPUObjects/MVKDevice.mm
index 9772757..83db387 100644
--- a/MoltenVK/MoltenVK/GPUObjects/MVKDevice.mm
+++ b/MoltenVK/MoltenVK/GPUObjects/MVKDevice.mm
@@ -2841,6 +2918,16 @@
 	mvkClear(&_vulkan14NoExtFeatures);		// Start with everything cleared
 	_vulkan14NoExtFeatures.pushDescriptor = true;
 
+	// FineWine: DXVK will not create a device without these, though Direct3D 9 (what FineWine
+	// uses DXVK for) has no geometry shaders and no cull distances. FineWine sets the variable
+	// only for programs it runs on DXVK; a shader that did use one would fail to convert.
+	const char* dxvk = getenv("FINEWINE_DXVK_FEATURES");
+	if (dxvk && dxvk[0] == '1') {
+		_features.geometryShader = true;
+		_features.shaderCullDistance = true;
+		_features.shaderFloat64 = true;
+		_features.depthClamp = true;
+	}
 }
 
 // Initializes the physical device property limits.
